#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9033537c-6596-540e-9bd3-d1cbeeabd1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151381} {:answer/id #uuid "e565a422-78a5-5156-95c3-37f3e40e3ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151382} {:answer/id #uuid "2784e1ac-6059-539c-84d8-e18877b6df64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151383} {:answer/id #uuid "394eb603-5869-540e-a54b-b403c5a2c6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151384} {:answer/id #uuid "704cc418-5f2d-5e6f-a447-641d3ab76e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9033537c-6596-540e-9bd3-d1cbeeabd1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151381} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc27ba3-e52d-57f2-a804-44b6bc1596df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e648a84-4fc0-5e89-9abf-3bfad9d2cf64" :question/math-academy-id "q-80266" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "For this question, $R$ must contain every point of $D$. Extending the integrand by zero on $R\\setminus D$ then preserves the integral: the function equals the original integrand throughout $D$ and contributes zero outside it. The rectangles must therefore be checked for containment, as explicitly required in the prompt.\n\nFirst, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/736575dceb6969f8b45e4038f702ebc84347ab66a1a8e5ea5103840ddd61ba31.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle I does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles II and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nThe ellipse has $-1\\le x\\le1$ and $-2\\le y\\le2$. Rectangle I excludes $(0,2)$, so it does not meet the containment requirement. II and III contain the entire bounding box of $D$, hence all of $D$.\n\nThe original unqualified integral equality is a weaker condition. Because $xy$ is odd in $x$ and both $D$ and each displayed $R\\cap D$ are symmetric under $x\\mapsto-x$, all three original rectangles give integrals equal to zero. Thus \"I, II, and III\" is correct for the literal original question. The added containment requirement makes \"II and III only\" correct for this study question."}]}
